Hmm, this doesn't trigger the bug because the text token's value includes the quotes. Let me think about this differently.

The issue is about the `stringifyName` function:

```typescript
function stringifyName(name: string, next: Token | undefined): string {
  if (!ID.test(name)) return quoteName(name);

  if (next?.type === "text" && ID_CONTINUE.test(next.value[0])) {
    return quoteName(name);
  }

  return name;
}
```

The first check `!ID.test(name)` - this uses the proper regex with `/u` flag, so it should correctly test the full name. If the name contains astral characters, this should work fine because regex with `/u` flag operates on code points.

The second check `ID_CONTINUE.test(next.value[0])` - this is the bug! `next.value[0]` returns only the first UTF-16 code unit (surrogate), which then fails the regex test.

But wait, in my test case, the text token value is `"𑀀"` (with quotes). Let me try a case where the text directly starts with the astral character.

Let me construct a case using a group or other construct that forces the tokens to be separate.

