Let me think about this more carefully:

In JavaScript, a string is a sequence of UTF-16 code units. An astral character (code point > 0xFFFF) is represented as a surrogate pair (two code units). When you access `str[0]`, you get the first code unit (the high surrogate). When the regex has the `/u` flag, it operates on Unicode code points. So when you test a lone surrogate against the regex, it won't match `ID_Continue` because a lone surrogate isn't a valid character.

The fix should use something like `[...next.value][0]` or `next.value.charAt(0)` with proper handling, or `String.fromCodePoint(next.value.codePointAt(0))`.


